//? Title: ZeroR: What a Majority-Class Baseline Measures
//? Subtitle: Empirical risk, held-out evaluation and the hidden cost of ignoring a minority
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
ZeroR predicts the most frequent training label for every input. We derive
its optimality among constant decisions under unweighted zero-one loss,
including why randomising a feature-free decision cannot improve that
objective. On `dm::datasets::titanic()`, the rule attains respectable
ordinary accuracy while missing every survivor. Training/test separation,
class-level recalls and balanced accuracy expose this difference. Repeated
splits illustrate sampling variation without changing the decision rule.
The result is a useful reference for a specified metric, rather than a
universal test of whether a model has learned anything.
)

\toc

> The data: Titanic survival is imbalanced

The built-in Titanic frame has 891 passengers and 12 columns. `Survived`
is an integer-coded nominal label: 0 for a passenger who died and 1 for
a survivor. ZeroR uses only this response, so missing ages, cabins or other
predictors do not remove training or evaluation rows. Numeric storage does
not make the response a regression target in this report.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto passengers = dm::datasets::titanic();
  std::print("{}\n", passengers.select({"PassengerId", "Survived", "Sex", "Age"}).head(8).to_md());
  std::print("\n| passengers | columns |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} |\n", passengers.nrows(), passengers.ncols());
}
```
