> The method: empirical risk for a constant decision

For labels \(y_1,\ldots,y_n\), let \(n_k\) count class \(k\). A
constant prediction \(a\) makes \(n-n_a\) mistakes. Its empirical
zero-one risk is therefore

$$
\widehat R(a)=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf1\{y_i\ne a\}
 =1-\frac{n_a}{n},\qquad
\widehat a\in\arg\max_k n_k.
$$
\alttext(The empirical error of a constant decision a is one minus the proportion of class a. Minimising this error selects the class with the largest count.)

The library chooses the smallest integer class in a tie. Its model stores
the selected class, its count and the training size. `accuracy()` is the
training fraction \(n_{\widehat a}/n\), not an estimate automatically
valid for another dataset.

Could a feature-free random decision improve training accuracy? Let
\(q_k\) be its probability of predicting class \(k\), independently of
the outcome. Since the \(q_k\) sum to one,

$$
\mathbb E[\widehat{\operatorname{accuracy}}]
 =\sum_k q_k\frac{n_k}{n}
 \le\max_k\frac{n_k}{n}.
$$
\alttext(The expected accuracy of a random constant classifier is a weighted average of class frequencies, so it cannot exceed their maximum.)

A deterministic majority decision attains the bound. This proves optimality
within the stated class of feature-free decisions and loss; it does not
prove that features contain no information, or that majority prediction
is optimal under another metric or cost.

The current implementation uses a sorted map for counts, so training costs
\(O(n\log K)\) time and \(O(K)\) counting storage for \(K\) classes.
The DataFrame adapter also holds an \(O(n)\) temporary label vector.
A prediction costs \(O(1)\) and does not inspect features. Empty training
input produces an empty low-level model; evaluation helpers reject that
model and empty evaluation data instead of reporting misleading scores.

> Fit on training data, then freeze the decision

The split is a reproducible, unstratified random 70/30 partition. This
keeps the numeric class column as supplied by the dataset. The model is
fitted on `train` only; the separate evaluation helpers do not recompute
the majority from `test`. A change in test prevalence can therefore change
accuracy even though the prediction stays fixed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::titanic(), 0.3, 2024);
  const auto model = dm::algorithms::zero_r_train(train, "Survived");
  std::print("Training class counts:\n\n{}\n", dm::algorithms::zero_r_counts(train, "Survived").to_md(6));
  std::print("\nTest class counts:\n\n{}\n", dm::algorithms::zero_r_counts(test, "Survived").to_md(6));
  std::print("\nFixed-model test evaluation:\n\n{}\n", dm::algorithms::zero_r_evaluate(model, test, "Survived").to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Training class counts:

| class | count | proportion |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 383 | 0.613782 |
| 1 | 241 | 0.386218 |


Test class counts:

| class | count | proportion |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 166 | 0.621723 |
| 1 | 101 | 0.378277 |


Fixed-model test evaluation:

| prediction | training_rows | training_accuracy | evaluated_rows | observed_classes | accuracy | balanced_accuracy |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 624 | 0.613782 | 267 | 2 | 0.621723 | 0.5 |
// result_end
\caption(The training and held-out class frequencies, followed by evaluation of the fixed training-majority prediction. Training accuracy is kept separate.)

The seed-2024 training set has 383 deaths and 241 survivors, so the frozen
prediction is zero. On 267 test passengers it is correct for 166 deaths:
ordinary accuracy is about 62.2%. The 101 test survivors are all missed.

> Why balanced accuracy reveals the blind spot

For evaluation class \(k\), recall is the fraction of its true members
predicted as \(k\). A constant rule has recall one for its chosen class
and zero for every other observed class. If that chosen class is present
among \(K\) evaluation classes, balanced accuracy is

$$
\operatorname{BA}=\frac1K\sum_{k=1}^{K}\operatorname{recall}_k
 =\frac1K.
$$
\alttext(Balanced accuracy is the mean of the class recalls. A constant prediction gets recall one for one present class and zero for the others, giving one divided by K.)

It is zero when the predicted class is absent. The helper averages only
over classes actually observed in evaluation; absent classes are not
invented as zero-recall members of the denominator. For this two-class
test set, the theoretical value is exactly 0.5, regardless of imbalance.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/zero_r_recalls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::titanic(), 0.3, 2024);
  const auto model = dm::algorithms::zero_r_train(train, "Survived");
  const auto confusion = dm::algorithms::zero_r_confusion(model, test, "Survived");
  std::print("{}\n", confusion.to_md(6));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(confusion.get_column("recall").values(), {"died (0)", "survived (1)"}, "recall")
      .abline_h(0.5, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1)
      .title("Every survivor is missed by the fixed majority rule")
      .y_label("held-out class recall").y_limits(0, 1.05)
      .size(850, 460).save_svg("build/zero_r_recalls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| class | support | predicted | true_positive | recall | precision |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 166 | 267 | 166 | 1 | 0.621723 |
| 1 | 101 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0.000000 |

\image(figures/zero_r_recalls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Class-level confusion summaries and recalls. The horizontal line is balanced accuracy, the mean of these two recalls. Zero predicted survivors give precision zero under the helper's explicit zero-denominator convention.)
\alttext(Recall is one for passengers who died and zero for survivors, while balanced accuracy is one half.)

If detecting survivors were the priority, a classifier could improve
survivor recall while losing some ordinary accuracy. That tradeoff would
need evaluation under the chosen objective. The statement that any model
below ZeroR accuracy has learned nothing would conceal it.

> Sampling variation across splits

For a fixed constant class, test accuracy equals its test-set prevalence.
If a test subset of size \(m\) is drawn without replacement from \(N\)
records with class proportion \(p\), the number of correct predictions
is hypergeometric and

$$
\mathbb E[\widehat A]=p,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat A)=\frac{p(1-p)}m\frac{N-m}{N-1}.
$$
\alttext(For a fixed class decision, expected test accuracy is its population proportion. Its variance has the binomial variance divided by test size, multiplied by the finite-population correction.)

This formula treats the decision as fixed. If a split changes the training
majority, selecting a new class changes the calculation. The following
runs record the chosen class explicitly. They reuse the same finite
passenger dataset; their spread is a split-sensitivity diagnostic, not
extra independent evidence about another population.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/zero_r_splits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto passengers = dm::datasets::titanic();
  std::vector<double> seeds, accuracy, balanced;
  std::print("| seed | chosen class | training accuracy | test accuracy | balanced accuracy |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int seed = 1; seed <= 20; ++seed) {
    const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(passengers, 0.3, seed);
    const auto model = dm::algorithms::zero_r_train(train, "Survived");
    const auto score = dm::algorithms::zero_r_evaluate(model, test, "Survived");
    seeds.push_back(seed); accuracy.push_back(score.double_at("accuracy", 0));
    balanced.push_back(score.double_at("balanced_accuracy", 0));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n", seed, model.prediction,
               model.accuracy(), accuracy.back(), balanced.back());
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(seeds, accuracy, "ordinary accuracy", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .points(seeds, balanced, "balanced accuracy", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 4)
      .title("Changing class proportions changes ordinary accuracy")
      .x_label("split seed").y_label("held-out score").y_limits(0.45, 0.75)
      .size(900, 480).save_svg("build/zero_r_splits.svg");
}
```
