\image(figures/zero_r_splits.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Twenty independently seeded partitions of the same data, showing the selected class and both held-out metrics.)
\alttext(Ordinary accuracy varies between splits while balanced accuracy stays at one half for the constant decision.)

All twenty fits choose class zero. Their test accuracies range from about
57.7% to 66.7%, while balanced accuracy remains exactly 0.5. This spread
comes from which class members enter each partition, without any feature
learning or change to the constant decision.

> Different losses give different feature-free baselines

With binary prevalence \(\pi=\Pr(Y=1)\), a false-positive cost
\(c_{01}\) and false-negative cost \(c_{10}\), the risks of constant
predictions zero and one are respectively \(\pi c_{10}\) and
\((1-\pi)c_{01}\). Predicting one is better when

$$
\pi>\frac{c_{01}}{c_{01}+c_{10}}.
$$
\alttext(The optimal constant positive decision under asymmetric costs uses a prevalence threshold equal to false-positive cost divided by the sum of the two error costs.)

This need not be the majority threshold of one half. Likewise, a constant
probability scored by squared probability error is optimised by the class
frequency, rather than a hard zero/one decision. ZeroR's hard classification
rule should not stand in for every probability or decision-cost baseline.

> Conclusion

The class counts explain the accuracy a constant rule can obtain. A
training-only fit gives an honest fixed decision for held-out evaluation;
per-class recall shows the minority it never detects. Balanced accuracy
checks the exact constant-rule value of one half, while repeated splits
show the effect of changing prevalence. The baseline is useful when its
loss, fitting data and evaluation metric are stated explicitly.

> References

- Weka. *ZeroR*, nominal-class majority rule (the numeric-target mean variant is not used here).
  https://weka.sourceforge.io/doc.stable/weka/classifiers/rules/ZeroR.html
- K. H. Brodersen, C. S. Ong, K. E. Stephan and J. M. Buhmann (2010).
  *The Balanced Accuracy and Its Posterior Distribution*. ICPR, 3121–3124.
  https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR.2010.764
